In mathematics, a manifold is a topological space thta locally resembles Euclidean space near each point. 
More precisely, an n-dimensional manifold, or n-mainifold for short, is a topolocgical space with the property that each point has a neighborhood that is hoemorphic to an open subset of n-dimensional Euclidean space. 

One-dimensional manifolds include lines and circles, but not self-crossing curves such as a figure-eight. 
Two-dimensional manifolds are also called surfaces. 
Examples include the plane, the sphere, and the torus, and also the klein bottle and real projective plane. 
